      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "intro_overview",
          "html": "<h3>Purpose</h3><p>This survey asks Wyoming citizens about a hard question: how should Wyoming handle abortion policy after the Wyoming Supreme Court's ruling?</p><p>In January 2026, the Wyoming Supreme Court ruled that the decision whether to terminate or continue a pregnancy is a health care decision protected by Article 1, Section 38 of the Wyoming Constitution.</p><p>We are Wyoming. Our representatives work for us. The Constitution belongs to the people, and every elected representative is bound by the oath of office to support, obey, and defend it.</p><p>Wyoming citizens hold sincere moral, legal, medical, and constitutional concerns on this issue. If Wyoming wants to change the legal framework, the honest path is a constitutional amendment placed before the people of Wyoming.</p><p>This survey asks whether Wyoming should follow that path, whether an amendment should define when human life begins and receives the protections of law, what safeguards should be addressed if an amendment is proposed, and whether our representatives should remain within the guardrails of the Constitution.</p><p><a class=\"button button--small button--secondary\" href=\"/assets/wy_health_care_constitutional_process_sources.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sources and background</a></p><h3>Privacy</h3><p>Please leave out private medical history, names of patients, names of providers, and identifying medical details. Results may be summarized publicly in aggregate form.</p><h3>How responses will be used</h3><ul><li>Results will be published in aggregate.</li><li>Exact question wording and response options will be published with the results.</li><li>Verified and unverified responses may be reported separately.</li><li>Individual responses will remain confidential.</li></ul>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
